;;   nbb os/aiueos/scripts/reproduce-kotoba-objects.cljs --amu <checkout> --attest
;;   nbb os/aiueos/scripts/sync-kernel-object-digests.cljs --write
;;   nbb os/aiueos/scripts/verify-loop-parameter-homes.cljs   ; expect exit 4
;;
;; and then the four entries below are deleted. Do not delete one without
;; re-running the checker: an entry removed on the strength of a compiler
;; version rather than a measurement is the assumption this file replaced.
;;
;; CLASSIFY WITH THE CHECKER, NOT WITH A REBUILD. `verify-loop-parameter-homes`
;; already answers "which committed objects carry this" for the whole tree --
;; 95 scanned, 301 self-tail loops, 4 findings, measured 2026-09-03 -- from
;; objdump alone: no compile, no amu, seconds. Comparing a rebuild at a
;; fix-containing pin against the committed bytes answers the same question
;; indirectly and costs a compile per object.
;;
;; It is also asymmetric, and only one direction is sound:
;;
;;   bytes DID NOT move   sound evidence the object is not at risk -- but it
;;                        assumes the fix is complete for the shape, which the
;;                        checker does not have to assume, because it looks for
;;                        the shape in the artifact.
;;   bytes DID move       NOT evidence of a defect. A codegen change moves
;;                        objects for every reason there is. Measured on the
;;                        earlier sweep at amu 370a04e0: 69 of 74 objects
;;                        changed bytes, while the shape is present in 4.
;;                        Reading movement as risk would flag 65 clean objects.
;;
;; So a rebuild comparison is a CORROBORATOR, and a good one when two methods
;; are made to agree. Worked example (QWEN-KERNELS-2, 2026-09-03):
;; `qwen35-attention.o` and `qwen35-recurrent-step.o` were built at
